<DocsLayout title="Validation Rounds" description="How thirteen rounds of generated reports, issue audits and fixes hardened the pool engine, the stack and the reports themselves" section="Testing" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/testing#results">Segmentation Validation</a> and services start/stop reports were not
    written once and trusted. Between 2 and 18&nbsp;October&nbsp;2026 they went through thirteen rounds (the systematic issue audits ran from round&nbsp;3, 16–18&nbsp;October): run the
    stack, export the PDF, audit the PDF line by line against the system's own event logs, write every
    discrepancy into an issues file, fix it, and run again. This page summarises what those rounds found. The raw
    material is kept in the repository's <code>reports/</code> folder, one numbered directory per round.
  </p>

  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="13 rounds" href="#rounds" icon="clock" tag="2–18 Oct" description="From a first pool test where a diverted session escaped to production, to two EXCLUSIVE verdicts on both deception layers." meta="2–18 Oct 2026" />
    <DocCard title="Stack bugs" href="#stack-findings" icon="alert" tag="Found by reports" description="Escapes via TLS GREASE, 502s after scale-down, pools wasted on dead sessions, containers killed on stop, logs shipped twice." meta="All fixed" />
    <DocCard title="Report bugs" href="#report-findings" icon="file" tag="Self-audit" description="Wrong owners, false failures, HTTP errors retried as network errors, short jobs missing from charts, mislabelled peaks." meta="Evidence quality" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="loop">The Report-Driven Loop</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Run.</strong> Start both stacks from the dashboard (each start and stop records its own services report), then run Segmentation Validation with 10 windows, some delayed so they attack only after pools were pre-built for them.</li>
    <li><strong>Audit.</strong> Read the exported PDF against independent sources — <code>pool_manager</code>'s event log (<code>honeypot_pool:events</code> in Redis), the pool registry, container logs and Elasticsearch — and mark each issue <em>confirmed</em> (seen in a log or registry) or <em>inferred</em> (from the PDF alone).</li>
    <li><strong>Record.</strong> Write the issues file next to the PDF (<code>pool_test_report_issues*.md</code>, <code>services_start_*.md</code>, …), grouped into stack problems, data correctness, charts and layout, each with a severity and a suggested fix.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix and track.</strong> Fix each item with tests, and add a status table to the issues file that names the commit — or says plainly why an item was only surfaced and not changed.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-run.</strong> The next round's file opens with what the previous fixes changed, then lists only new issues.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">Why audit a report against the logs</div>
    <p>A report that only restates what the test harness believed cannot catch the harness being wrong. Most of
    the high-severity stack bugs below were found because a number in the PDF disagreed with
    <code>pool_manager</code>'s own event log — a hand-off to the wrong session, a pool that was never released,
    a build counted as a spare.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rounds">Round by Round</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Round</th><th>Reports</th><th>Main findings</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Exploit report, five-window pool tests, start-ups (2–7 Oct)</td><td>First scaling runs; under a memory cap (1,058&nbsp;MB free, 2,560&nbsp;MB needed) pools were shared round-robin — verdict SHARED EXPECTED</td><td>Baseline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Start/stop with and without purge, pool tests with 5 and 10 windows, idle browsing (9 Oct)</td><td>Five windows each on their own pool (EXCLUSIVE); first delayed-window runs</td><td>Material shared with the supervisor</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Pool test, SIEM and edge start-ups</td><td>A diverted window escaped to production by clearing cookies (TLS GREASE); a new pool handed to an abandoned session; budget stopped at a quarter of real use; the edge shipped no logs; Kibana dashboards never seeded</td><td>~35 items fixed or surfaced</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Start/stop of both stacks, operator notes on the pool test</td><td>Event times wrong (created ≠ started, removed ≠ exited), sample gaps drawn as lines, stale screenshots; slow 3-pool pre-build (213&nbsp;s); CPU shown as % of one core</td><td><code>POOL_FAST_CLONE</code>, CPU normalised to all cores, memory breakdown table</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Pool test (verdict EXCLUSIVE)</td><td><code>ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED</code> during builds; 502 after scale-down; post-run polling kept pools busy; reverse-proxy traces logged and shipped twice</td><td>Router fixed; report learned to detect 502s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Pool test</td><td>No 502, reassignment HTTP 200; but the reassignment check leaked into the run's statistics and rebuilt pools shown as "failed"</td><td>Separate observation phase, clean-up after it</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>Pool test, edge start-up</td><td>HTTP 4xx answers retried as failed loads, re-sending each attack up to ~67 times; short borrows missed by routing samples</td><td>Retry only on connection errors</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>Pool test, edge stop</td><td>Tamper schedule diverted a delayed window before its own attack; tables split before screenshot rows</td><td>One forgery per window, pagination fixes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>Pool test, edge start and stop</td><td>Black screenshots of near-empty pages; five containers SIGKILLed after 10&nbsp;s on stop; nginx <code>[info]</code> lines counted as errors</td><td><code>init: true</code>, level-aware error matching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>Pool test (7 delayed), edge start and stop</td><td>Header-only screenshots from small views; one-shot jobs too short to sample; stop now 8&nbsp;s instead of 24&nbsp;s</td><td><code>docker events</code> for job timing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>First database-proxy-level pool test, start, stop</td><td>Verdict EXCLUSIVE on the database layer; builds of 13–17&nbsp;s</td><td>Second layer validated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>12</td><td>Dashboard gap analyses (two rounds)</td><td>No Overview page while its data was collected and discarded; no native event feed, session drill-down, Suricata page, alerting or pool view; then alerting blind to pipeline/pool/Suricata health</td><td>Sidebar grew from 13 to 20 pages</td></tr>
    <tr><td>13</td><td>Pool test, both start-ups</td><td>Re-validation of segmentation on the WordPress-proxy level</td><td>Verdict EXCLUSIVE</td></tr>
  </table>

