  <h2 id="stack-findings">What the Reports Found in the Stack</h2>

  <h3 id="session">Session redirection</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Escape by clearing cookies.</strong> A window diverted at 1:20 cleared its site data and spent about four minutes on the real shop as a new session. Chromium (QtWebEngine) puts a random GREASE value in each TLS ClientHello, so the passive recovery fingerprint changed per connection. Fixed with <code>session_identity_rules.strip_grease()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Pools handed to sessions nobody uses.</strong> The hand-off took the oldest entry of the waiting queue, and a session whose client had vanished kept its place for the 24&nbsp;h assignment TTL. A live window waited 99&nbsp;s instead of ~18&nbsp;s. Fixed: a waiting session gets a pool only if its client sent a request within <code>POOL_WAITING_ACTIVE_SECONDS</code> (default 60).</li>
    <li><strong>502 after scale-down.</strong> Every nginx worker cached session → pool for 300&nbsp;s, and a pool with a missing or stale health status was treated as healthy, so requests went to a removed container. Fixed: a cached pool not in the ready list is a cache miss.</li>
    <li><strong>Tamper score diverting the wrong window.</strong> A delayed window received three tamperings in a row; two rejected forgeries (+50 each) reached the threshold before its attack. The test now spreads tamper events across windows and forges each window's cookie at most once — tampering alone must not divert.</li>
    <li><strong>The test's own setup scored as suspicious.</strong> The window that looked up product IDs through the Store API reached score 65 before attacking, close to the threshold.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="scaling">Resource delegation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Budget by reservation, not by use.</strong> Scaling stopped at 8 pools with ~3.4&nbsp;GB used of a 12&nbsp;GB budget. <code>POOL_RESERVE_MEM_MB</code> / <code>POOL_RESERVE_CPUS</code> now set the per-pool charge; the host-level checks still stop growth if the host really runs short.</li>
    <li><strong>A spare built before the compose pools existed.</strong> Before pools 1–3 became healthy, <code>pool_manager</code> saw zero free pools and built a runtime spare, taking budget and adding a 60&nbsp;s build to the busiest part of the start. Compose pools that are still starting now count as on their way.</li>
    <li><strong>Scale-down left abandoned sessions' pools running.</strong> The forced scale-down released only each window's current session. It now releases every session a window held during the run.</li>
    <li><strong>Slow builds.</strong> A runtime pool already clones the configured production database, then re-applied the same storefront settings via wp-cli. <code>POOL_FAST_CLONE</code> (experimental, off by default) skips that step; raising <code>POOL_PARALLEL_BUILDS</code> builds several delayed windows' pools in one wave.</li>
    <li><strong>Network churn.</strong> Each build adds containers and veth interfaces, and Chromium cancels in-flight loads on every interface change (<code>ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED</code>). Every such load succeeds on retry; in round 10 all 21 failures fell inside the 7-pool pre-build while windows kept browsing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="lifecycle">Start-up and shutdown</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kibana dashboards never seeded.</strong> Kibana's healthcheck accepted the <code>200 OK</code> of its temporary <em>preboot</em> server, the import got 503, <code>curl</code> without <code>--fail</code> exited 0, and cleanup deleted the failed job. Fixed with a stricter healthcheck and an import job that waits, retries and verifies; failed one-shot jobs are kept visible.</li>
    <li><strong>Kibana at its memory limit.</strong> Kibana sat at exactly 1,024&nbsp;MiB; <code>KIBANA_MEM_LIMIT</code> is now 2&nbsp;GiB.</li>
    <li><strong>Host settings.</strong> Redis warned that background saves may fail without <code>vm.overcommit_memory=1</code> — and a failed save makes Redis refuse writes, which would stop sessions, threat state and the pool registry together. The dashboard now checks this and Elasticsearch's <code>vm.max_map_count</code> on every host (<code>core/host_settings.py</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Containers killed on stop.</strong> <code>backup_service</code>, <code>log_capper</code>, <code>honeypot_content_sync</code>, <code>pool_manager</code> and <code>session_store</code> ran a shell or Python as PID&nbsp;1, which ignores SIGTERM without a handler, so Docker killed them after 10&nbsp;s. <code>init: true</code> and <code>exec redis-server</code> cut <code>down</code> from 24&nbsp;s to 8&nbsp;s.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="logging">Logging and the SIEM</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The edge shipped nothing.</strong> <code>vector_outbound</code> belongs to the <code>elk</code> profile, but the dashboard always started compose with <code>--profile '*'</code>, so it ran against the placeholder <code>VECTOR_HOST</code>, failed DNS and dropped events while the SIEM held 0 <code>honeypot-*</code> documents. The dashboard now scales the shipper to 0 while shipping isn't configured. On one machine, point <code>VECTOR_HOST</code> at <code>host.docker.internal</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Request traces shipped twice.</strong> nginx writes <code>info</code>-level traces both to <code>error.log</code> and to stdout, and Vector read both, so every line was parsed and shipped twice. Vector also tagged every <code>error.log</code> line as an error; it now carries the line's own level.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-run traffic.</strong> After a report finished, each window's cart poll kept sending ~3 requests per second, keeping pools busy so idle scale-down never fired. Polling now stops with the run.</li>
    <li><strong>Pre-warm noise.</strong> The proxy logged its pre-warm pass ~160 times per start, and the word "failed" in "0 failed" raised false warnings. It now logs one summary line, and error matching trusts nginx's own level tags.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="report-findings">What the Reports Found in Themselves</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Effect</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP error answers treated as failed loads</td><td>QtWebEngine reports a 4xx page as <code>loadFinished(False)</code>, so a 404 attack was re-sent up to ~67 times, inflating scores and evidence</td><td>Retry only connection-level errors; never retry an attack after an HTTP answer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Timeline merged a window's sessions</td><td>A window that lost its session was credited with outcomes of its old one</td><td>Timelines and cases per <code>(window, session)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Owner = last window seen</td><td>Designed borrowing reported as cross-traffic leaks</td><td>Owner = first holder; explained borrows marked as expected</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pools shared with an abandoned session drawn green</td><td>Verdict counted only test windows</td><td>Shared when the Redis owner count exceeds the windows on it</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reassignment check counted in the run</td><td>False "borrowed" findings and builds shown as failed</td><td>Run ends at the scale-down request; the check is its own phase</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Started" meant created, "stopped" meant removed</td><td>Start order and job durations untrustworthy</td><td>Times from <code>docker events</code> / <code>docker inspect</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>One-shot jobs shorter than the ~1.5&nbsp;s poll</td><td>Jobs silently missing from charts</td><td>Follow <code>docker events</code>; list jobs too brief to sample</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Killed containers reported as clean</td><td>Exit code 137 gone once compose removed the container</td><td>Exit codes from die events</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sample gaps drawn as straight lines; lines ramping from 0 before a container existed</td><td>Invented load</td><td>Lines broken at gaps, start at the first real sample</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Host CPU as % of one core; the RAM peak labelled as CPU peak</td><td>Misleading load figures</td><td>% of all logical CPUs, one decimal; peak per series</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Screenshots: stale, black, or header-only</td><td>Evidence that did not show the claim</td><td>Wait for a painted frame; minimum view size during export</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="lessons">Lessons</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fingerprints must survive the client's own randomisation.</strong> A recovery fingerprint that hashes GREASE is not a fingerprint.</li>
    <li><strong>Every cache needs an invalidation path.</strong> The 502s came from a worker-local route cache that a pool removal never touched.</li>
    <li><strong>Queues need liveness.</strong> A waiting list without a last-seen time hands resources to clients that have gone.</li>
    <li><strong>Budget on what is used, cap on what is allowed.</strong> Charging container limits fitted a quarter of the pools the host could carry.</li>
    <li><strong>Tell server answers from network failures.</strong> Retrying a 404 changes the experiment.</li>
    <li><strong>PID&nbsp;1 is special.</strong> Without <code>init: true</code> or <code>exec</code>, SIGTERM is ignored and every stop costs the full grace period.</li>
    <li><strong>A test harness can be the adversary.</strong> Setup requests, post-run polling and tamper scheduling all changed scores or load until they were isolated.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
